-- T6 regression: temporal-context leakage through thunk memoization (basic).
--
-- A shared module-level binding whose force reads the temporal context
-- (TODAY/NOW) must be re-evaluated when served under a DIFFERENT context.
-- Pre-fix, the first force's value was memoized unconditionally and served
-- stale in BOTH directions (forced inside an `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` scope
-- and read outside, and vice versa), through nested scopes, and through
-- transitive dependencies.
--
-- The test harness pins the clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z,
-- so ambient TODAY = DATE OF 31, 1, 2025.
--
-- (The tz-axis analogue — a thunk forced before a TIMEZONE IS decl being
-- invalidated after it — is not observable in a batch run because all
-- directives evaluate after all module decls; it is covered by the
-- validFor unit tests in TemporalContextSpec.)

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 1: inside-to-outside (forced under the override first)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`today snapshot` MEANS TODAY

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 1, 2020 (forced under the override; correct)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) `today snapshot`

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 1, 2025 (ambient; pre-fix leaked DATE OF 1, 1, 2020)
#EVAL `today snapshot`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 2: outside-to-inside (forced ambiently first)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`today snapshot 2` MEANS TODAY

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 1, 2025 (ambient force; memoized)
#EVAL `today snapshot 2`

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 1, 2020 (pre-fix leaked the ambient DATE OF 31, 1, 2025)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) `today snapshot 2`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 3: nested overrides (2020 wrapping 2010; forced innermost first)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`today snapshot 3` MEANS TODAY

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 1, 2010 (innermost override wins)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2010) `today snapshot 3`)

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 1, 2020 (pre-fix leaked DATE OF 1, 1, 2010 one level up)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) `today snapshot 3`

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 1, 2025 (pre-fix leaked DATE OF 1, 1, 2010 to ambient)
#EVAL `today snapshot 3`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 4: transitive dependency (the thunk's head is not a nullary builtin;
-- context-dependence must be detected dynamically during the force)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`day after today` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL TODAY PLUS 1)

-- expected: DATE OF 2, 1, 2020 (day after overridden today)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) `day after today`

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 2, 2025 (pre-fix leaked DATE OF 2, 1, 2020)
#EVAL `day after today`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 5: NOW / DATETIME (sub-day precision of tcSystemTime, plus the
-- tcDocumentTimezone axis in the fingerprint)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`now snapshot` MEANS NOW

-- expected: 2020-01-01T00:00:00+0000 (midnight UTC of the override serial)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) `now snapshot`

-- expected: 2025-01-31T15:45:30+0000 (pre-fix leaked the 2020 instant)
#EVAL `now snapshot`
